# infra/pulse-default.pa — PulseAudio runtime config for cloud-browser-webrtc
#
# This file is COPYed in the Dockerfile to /etc/pulse/default.pa, replacing
# the Debian default. PulseAudio executes default.pa automatically on
# daemon startup, so this is the entire bootstrap script — no -F flag in
# the supervisord command.
#
# Audio routing (see infra/audio-routing.md for the full diagram):
#
#     Chromium playback ──> cb_audio (null sink)
#                             │
#                             └─ cb_audio.monitor ──┐
#                                                   │ module-loopback
#                                                   ▼
#                              cb_capture (null sink) ──> cb_capture.monitor
#                                                            │
#                                            getUserMedia ───┘
#                                              (default source)
#
# The two-sink layout exists so that:
#   - Chromium's default sink (cb_audio) is a clean playback target.
#   - The default source (cb_capture.monitor) carries whatever Chromium
#     just played, which is what `getUserMedia({audio:true})` and the
#     headless-side capture for `getDisplayMedia({audio:true})` will
#     read on Linux/PulseAudio.
#   - Pointing the default source at a *separate* sink's monitor (rather
#     than cb_audio.monitor directly) avoids a self-feedback loop if any
#     module ever inadvertently routes captured audio back to the default
#     sink.
#
# `virtual_mic` remains as an explicit silent null-source for tests that
# specifically want a quiet microphone (e.g. getUserMedia tests that
# don't want to pick up streamer-page audio).
#
# `.fail` makes startup abort if any module fails to load — better to
# crashloop than to silently run without audio plumbing.

.fail

### IPC: native socket inside the cbuser runtime dir. auth-anonymous=1 is
### safe here because the socket lives at 0700 perms under
### /run/user/1000/pulse and only cbuser-owned processes can reach it.
load-module module-native-protocol-unix auth-anonymous=1 socket=/run/user/1000/pulse/native

### Playback sink Chromium writes into. Named cb_audio per the T24 contract;
### everything downstream of audio capture refers to this name.
load-module module-null-sink sink_name=cb_audio sink_properties=device.description="cb-audio-playback"

### Capture sink — receives a loopback of cb_audio.monitor. Its own monitor
### is what we expose as the default source so getUserMedia/getDisplayMedia
### audio captures everything Chromium plays.
load-module module-null-sink sink_name=cb_capture sink_properties=device.description="cb-audio-capture"

### Loopback: cb_audio.monitor -> cb_capture. latency_msec=20 keeps the
### relay tight without spending CPU on tiny chunks. *_dont_move=true
### freezes the routing so an external `pactl move-source-output` can't
### silently break the capture path.
load-module module-loopback source=cb_audio.monitor sink=cb_capture latency_msec=20 source_dont_move=true sink_dont_move=true

### Optional silent microphone for tests that want a quiet input device
### independent of whatever cb_audio is playing.
load-module module-null-source source_name=virtual_mic source_properties=device.description="cbrowser-virtual-mic"

set-default-sink cb_audio
set-default-source cb_capture.monitor
